{"title":"2002 AMC 12P Problems/Problem 16","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The altitudes of a triangle are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/4/3240fb2ac093a9395c69745eb463859532bf1189.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12, 15,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/0/0f06afca5b285202e10a200dc2a833602490ab73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" /> The largest angle in this triangle is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/8/a08181afdb14ce2fd61cc7d07c6e2d78ab712285.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A) }72^\\circ \\qquad \\text{(B) }75^\\circ \\qquad \\text{(C) }90^\\circ \\qquad \\text{(D) }108^\\circ \\qquad \\text{(E) }120^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"434\" height=\"19\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/7/9f73d811eca6419d9c9a27423331d71ff06eb957.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"29\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> denote the bases of altitudes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/4/3240fb2ac093a9395c69745eb463859532bf1189.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12, 15,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/5/d859b55509f630845eb1bec092041feb5b3fe460.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"16\" /> respectively. Since they are all altitudes and bases of the same triangle, they have the same area, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/b/9bbbcf8b74ba270122bee691b155dbfc0c29812c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{12a}{2}=\\frac{15b}{2}=\\frac{20c}{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"147\" height=\"39\" /> Multiplying by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/e/6fe49efe3eba756ac3a9fe73121d9a8e73e74331.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12a=15b=20c.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"135\" height=\"14\" /> Notice that a simple solution to the equation is if all of them equal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/1/2e190635a7b37a6aba2f7526f8eca863ca365e98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12 \\cdot 15 \\cdot 20.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" /> That means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/f/d2f255d2c069b72b8e8873b7f015550df3247d3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=15 \\cdot 20, b=12 \\cdot 20,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6e0964963c9cc54cbddf2b5469b8302fe645188.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=12 \\cdot 15.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" /> Simplifying our solution to check for Pythagorean triples we see that this is just a Pythagorean triple, namely a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/1/af1a2cb4ff1720f169ddde1726c1a5a1ab816ae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3-4-5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" /> triangle. Since the other two angles of a right triangle must be acute, the right angle must be the greatest angle. Therefore, our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/c/8cc6b16d4d6bfa78be65d8168919d5262fcbfe53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }90^\\circ}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"79\" height=\"30\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P\" title=\"2002 AMC 12P\">2002 AMC 12P</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems\" title=\"2002 AMC 12P Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Answer_Key\" title=\"2002 AMC 12P Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2002 AMC 12P Problems/Problem 15\">Problem 15</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2002 AMC 12P Problems/Problem 17\">Problem 17</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?tit